window.KB_PUBLIC = {"generated":"2026-11-05","sources":[{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","title":"Agentic Artificial Intelligence from the perspective of data protection, V1.1","publisher":"Agencia Española de Protección de Datos (AEPD)","date":"2026-02","year":"2026","category":"Datenschutz","url":"https://www.aepd.es/en/guides/agentic-artificial-intelligence.pdf","binding":"Empfehlung (non-binding guidance of a supervisory authority; the text calls itself \"an introductory study without pretensions to be exhaustive\", PDF p. 70)","dateNote":"Version 1.1, February 2026 (as stated on the cover)","note":"","abstract":"A 71-page guidance paper from the Spanish data protection authority on what changes in a personal data processing when it is carried out fully or partly by AI agents.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>A 71-page guidance paper from the Spanish data protection authority on what changes in a personal data processing when it is carried out fully or partly by AI agents. It describes agent architecture, vulnerabilities, GDPR compliance questions, threats such as prompt injection and memory poisoning, and a catalogue of measures. Its main position is that the agent is only a means of processing, so the controller stays fully responsible and must govern, minimise, limit autonomy, log and supervise. It rejects both irrational rejection and uncritical acceptance of agents (PDF p. 2).</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Role clarification: the controller determines purposes and means \"regardless of the form\" of the means, including agentic systems, and must ensure compliance and manage the new risks (PDF p. 31). A provider of an agentic service that processes personal data in the framework of the controller's processing acts as processor (PDF p. 33). Third-party services receiving personal data are processors; services that receive no personal data or cannot link it to a user have no data protection role (PDF p. 32).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: before an agent touches client personal data, document each service in the chain with its data protection role and the contract, and design and document the data flows (PDF p. 33, accountability).</li>\n<li>Transparency: additional recipients, for example a generative AI service of another entity, must be named to data subjects and users, storage periods and additional automated decisions must be informed (PDF p. 34). Further processing for a new purpose needs prior information under Art. 13(2) GDPR (PDF p. 34).</li>\n<li>Lawfulness: if the agent adds no processing beyond the original, no new basis is needed; if it does, a basis and for special categories an Art. 9 exception are required (PDF p. 35). The use of an agent \"is not a purpose in itself\" (PDF p. 35).</li>\n<li>The RoPA must be updated when agents replace traditional means: categories, recipients, transfers, retention, Art. 32 measures (PDF p. 36).</li>\n<li>Data subject rights must remain exercisable; memory and logs must be technically able to support access, rectification, erasure, restriction and objection by design (PDF pp. 26, 36 to 37).</li>\n<li>Art. 22 GDPR: agents do not always produce automated decisions, but where they do, the conditions, safeguards and information duties apply (PDF pp. 37 to 38). Other automated actions such as sending mail or transferring files can affect confidentiality and call for reversibility by design (PDF p. 38).</li>\n<li>International transfers that arise through agents need Chapter V safeguards and entry in the RoPA; without them the agent should be redesigned or replaced (PDF pp. 42 to 43).</li>\n<li>A DPIA is not required in every case but depends on the processing and agent type; an existing DPIA must be reviewed (PDF p. 41).</li>\n<li>An agent must not give consents or enter contracts on behalf of a user without control (PDF pp. 30, 33).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<p>The source says nothing specific on research, research integrity, funding applications or peer review. Its general points on hallucination-free sources are limited to quality of retrieved external information (PDF p. 20) and are framed as data protection accuracy issues.</p>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Rule of 2, in its AI agent formulation: an agent should not combine all three properties of processing untrusted inputs, access to sensitive information and the ability to act automatically. Allowed pairings are: with untrusted input and sensitive access, no automatic action without human supervision; with sensitive access and action, guarantees of integrity are needed; with untrusted input and action, no access to sensitive information or personal data (PDF pp. 39 to 40). The figure itself is not readable in the extracted text; the three cases are described in the running text.</li>\n<li>Prompt injection: direct and indirect, including instructions hidden in PDFs, emails or web pages (PDF p. 49). Named variants include memory poisoning and RAG poisoning, zero-click injection, data exfiltration through URL parameters, session hijacking and lateral movement, long pipeline attacks, context confusion, delayed triggers, privilege escalation through tools, attacks on workflow platforms, screen control and deletion or ransomware (PDF pp. 49 to 50).</li>\n<li>Controls named: strict separation of data and instructions, labelling and traceability of content origin, limited tool privileges, validation and sanitisation of inputs at each stage, guardrails (PDF p. 61); service catalogue and whitelists with versions (PDF p. 61); restriction of accessible services to what a processing needs (PDF p. 61); guardrails and rigid formats on tool parameters and on tool responses (PDF p. 62).</li>\n<li>Human checkpoints: defined criteria for approval before high-impact actions, irreversible actions such as permanent deletion, sending communications or payments, atypical behaviour, and user-defined limits (PDF p. 62). Reversibility of agent actions should be assessed (PDF p. 62). Adjustable autonomy per processing (PDF p. 62).</li>\n<li>Identity and privileges: least privilege, granular RBAC/ABAC, MFA for high-privilege accounts, limited credential lifetime, no delegation of privileges between agents except in predefined flows, mutual authentication between agents (PDF p. 66). Excessive permissions make an agent a pivot point between systems (PDF p. 30).</li>\n<li>Sandboxing: containers or microVMs and restricted terminals with limited commands, services and network access, \"essential in the deployment test phases\" (PDF p. 67).</li>\n<li>Circuit breakers and hard step limits for loops, goal deviation and mass data access (PDF p. 68). Data extraction flow control requiring express user action for mass shipments to third parties (PDF p. 67).</li>\n<li>Update control with the power to decide when updates go to production, version control with roll-back (PDF pp. 66 to 67).</li>\n<li>Repeatability: record configuration of decision processes including inputs, intermediate data traffic and pseudo-randomness settings such as seeds and temperature (PDF p. 66). The source states that agent behaviour is non-repeatable without control of sources, services, versions and memory (PDF pp. 29 to 30).</li>\n<li>Verification and validation tests remain applicable; golden tests with reference results to detect deviations (PDF pp. 54, 65).</li>\n<li>A standard operating procedure for prompts with structured fields (PDF pp. 65 to 66).</li>\n<li>Easy low-code agent tools (n8n is shown) tempt unqualified users to deploy outside governance, described as \"BYOAgentic\" (PDF p. 21).</li>\n<li>Candidate rules for DHCraft agents: no combination of untrusted input, sensitive access and autonomous action without a human gate, least privilege per task, sandboxed execution, whitelisted tools, step limits, and log of inputs, tool calls and versions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A governance framework that includes agentic AI \"is the most important measure\" (PDF p. 51). Its elements include assigned roles, selection criteria for agents and connections, redesign control of processing, human supervision, formalised relationships with model and service providers, monitoring and retirement, incident management and training (PDF pp. 52 to 53).</li>\n<li>Accept the possibility of failure: design processing and reaction plans on the principle of safe failure (PDF p. 52).</li>\n<li>Misuse of the human as scapegoat: responsibility must not be shifted to the supervisor; the user and supervisor have assigned responsibility within limits, but it does not replace the controller's diligence in design and tool selection (PDF p. 47). Automation bias can undermine human supervision (PDF pp. 28, 47 to 48).</li>\n<li>Effective human supervision requires competence and authority, training, independence, diligence, means, information and time. A four-eyes principle may apply for high-impact processes (PDF pp. 63 to 64).</li>\n<li>Memory control: compartmentalise memory by processing, case and user, retention limits, option to disable persistent memory, sanitisation, no-log or zero-retention policy at component level (PDF pp. 58 to 60).</li>\n<li>Documentation as an integrity-controlled, dynamically maintained record of decisions, architecture and events; traceability and data lineage (PDF pp. 64 to 65).</li>\n<li>Qualified professionals needed for deployment (PDF p. 64). Literacy at management, ICT and user levels, and the role of the DPO (PDF p. 69).</li>\n<li>Evidence-based continuous assessment, automated where the processing is automated (PDF p. 53), and avoidance of \"checkbox security\" (PDF p. 51). Incremental, precautionary rollout starting with low-risk uses (PDF p. 55).</li>\n<li>Contracts and terms of service of services change unilaterally and must be evaluated at design and over the life cycle (PDF p. 54).</li>\n<li>The paper recommends the LIINE4DU threat modelling framework (PDF p. 38), refers to the IMDA Singapore governance framework as a guide (PDF p. 52, footnote 39) and notes that the AI Act or other regulations may also apply depending on entity and system (PDF p. 8, 52).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-binding guidance of the Spanish authority, not an EDPB text; the Austrian authority is not mentioned.</li>\n<li>Scope is data protection of personal data processing by controllers and processors; it explicitly excludes domestic use, model development and training, and organisations where no personal data is processed (PDF p. 8).</li>\n<li>It does not analyse any concrete processing and describes risks as not inherent to all agents (PDF pp. 9, 17).</li>\n<li>No guidance for research integrity, funding, publication or software licensing questions.</li>\n<li>The Rule of 2 figure is not in the extracted text, so its wording is reconstructed from the explanatory paragraphs.</li>\n<li>The paper is general and rarely distinguishes small organisations; many measures assume an organisation with a DPO and an ICT department.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-2","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-3","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-4","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-5","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-6","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-13","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-18","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-19","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-30"]},{"id":"Art29WP_2017-04-05_wp243-guidelines-dpo","title":"Guidelines on Data Protection Officers ('DPOs'), WP 243 rev.01","publisher":"Article 29 Data Protection Working Party","date":"2017-04-05 (exception)","year":"2017","category":"Datenschutz","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/newsroom/article29/redirection/document/44100","binding":"Empfehlung (guidance)","dateNote":"Adopted 13 December 2016, as last revised and adopted 5 April 2017","note":"Included as an approved exception to the 2026 date window, for the question of designating a data protection officer.","abstract":"Designation is mandatory in the three cases of Art. 37(1): public authority or body, core activities involving regular and systematic monitoring on a large scale, and core activities involving large-scale processing of special categories or criminal-conviction data (PDF p. 5).","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Designation is mandatory in the three cases of Art. 37(1): public authority or body, core activities involving regular and systematic monitoring on a large scale, and core activities involving large-scale processing of special categories or criminal-conviction data (PDF p. 5).</li>\n<li>\"Core activities\" are the key operations needed to reach the organisation's goals, and include activities where processing is an inextricable part, while support functions such as payroll or standard IT support are usually ancillary (PDF p. 7).</li>\n<li>\"Large scale\" has no fixed number. The Working Party names four factors: number of data subjects, volume and range of data, duration, and geographical extent (PDF p. 7 to 8).</li>\n<li>Art. 37 applies to controllers and processors. A processor serving many small clients can reach large scale taken together, while a controller that meets the criteria does not automatically oblige its processor (PDF p. 9 to 10).</li>\n<li>Organisations should document their internal analysis of whether a DPO is required, and a voluntary designation is bound by Arts. 37 to 39 as if it were mandatory (PDF p. 5).</li>\n<li>A person with data protection tasks who is not a DPO must not carry that title (PDF p. 6).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Examples of large-scale processing include patient data at a hospital, geo-location data processed by a processor specialised in such services, and data processing by telecoms providers. Examples of non-large-scale processing are patient data of an individual physician and conviction data of an individual lawyer (PDF p. 8).</li>\n<li>A processor \"having many customers\" can be large scale in aggregate (processor example, PDF p. 9 to 10). A small firm with few archive clients is not comparable on the text, which is an inference.</li>\n<li>The DPO of a processor also oversees the processor's own controller activities such as HR and IT (PDF p. 10).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Footnote 14 cites Recital 91 and notes a large grey zone between the extremes of an individual professional and whole-country processing (PDF p. 7).</li>\n<li>Special categories include data on racial or ethnic origin, religious or philosophical beliefs, health and biometric data for identification (PDF p. 5, footnote 7). Testimony recordings may touch several of them, which is an inference.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Tracking and profiling are examples of monitoring, and the notion is not limited to the online environment (PDF p. 8). Agent tooling that profiles individuals would be assessed against this, which is an inference.</li>\n<li>Standard IT support is usually ancillary (PDF p. 7).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep a written analysis of the designation question and update it when new activities or services start (PDF p. 5).</li>\n<li>A voluntary DPO may be an employee or external under a service contract, and must hold no role that determines purposes and means of processing, for example senior management or head of IT (PDF p. 12, p. 16).</li>\n<li>Staff or consultants who advise on data protection without being DPOs must have a clearly different title (PDF p. 6).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Guidance of the Article 29 Working Party, not binding law. The document text does not itself state the later endorsement by the EDPB.</li>\n<li>It predates the application of the GDPR (25 May 2018) and does not address generative AI or agentic systems.</li>\n<li>It gives no numeric thresholds and no rule for very small organisations.</li>\n<li>Section 3 on position and tasks was read only for conflicts of interest and external DPOs.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-27"]},{"id":"arXiv_2026-01-21_failed-agentic-pull-requests","title":"Where Do AI Coding Agents Fail? An Empirical Study of Failed Agentic Pull Requests in GitHub (preprint)","publisher":"Drexel University and Missouri S&T, via arXiv","date":"2026-01-21","year":"2026","category":"Empirische Studien","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15195","binding":"Befund (empirical finding)","dateNote":"21 January 2026 (arXiv stamp, PDF p. 1)","note":"","abstract":"This is a mining study of 33,596 pull requests (PRs) authored by five coding agents (OpenAI Codex, Copilot, Devin, Cursor, Claude Code) on GitHub projects with more than 100 stars, taken from the AIDev-pop dataset (PDF pp. 1-3).","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>This is a mining study of 33,596 pull requests (PRs) authored by five coding agents (OpenAI Codex, Copilot, Devin, Cursor, Claude Code) on GitHub projects with more than 100 stars, taken from the AIDev-pop dataset (PDF pp. 1-3). The quantitative part compares merged and not-merged PRs on task type, size, CI results and review activity. The qualitative part codes the primary rejection reason of a stratified sample of 600 rejected PRs into a taxonomy (562 were still accessible). The authors report that documentation, CI and build tasks merge most often, performance and bug-fix tasks least often, and that the most frequent rejection pattern is that nobody reviewed the PR (PDF pp. 2-4). The paper is a preprint and a short five-page study.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Where DHCraft agents contribute to a client's or third party's repository, the findings suggest following the project's contribution norms. Maintainers' comments ask for small, focused PRs limited to one coherent change (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Licence and ownership problems appeared in a small number of rejections, with agents not meeting requirements such as signing a Contributor License Agreement. The authors read this as a legal and governance constraint that current agents cannot satisfy (4 PRs, PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Duplicate PRs were the most common reason for rejection among PRs that were actively reviewed (142 of 562, 23%), which indicates a need to check for existing work before opening a PR (PDF p. 4).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Not-merged PRs involve larger changes in lines and files, with small-to-medium effect sizes (Cliff's delta 0.17 for lines and 0.10 for files), and they more often fail CI (delta 0.24) (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>In the logistic regression each additional failed CI check lowers the odds of a merge by about 15% (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Merge rates differ by task type. Across agents, documentation (84%), CI (79%) and build (74%) lead, and performance (55%) and fix (64%) trail (PDF p. 3). Merge rates also differ by agent, from 82.59% for Codex to 43.04% for Copilot (PDF p. 3). The authors do not control for differences in how the agents are used, so the agent ranking is descriptive.</li>\n<li>Code-level rejections were mostly CI or test failures (99 of 562, 17%), followed by incorrect (19) and incomplete (15) implementations (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>The conclusion recommends that agents validate submissions against CI before opening a PR and decompose tasks into localised changes (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>A small group of rejections (9) involved agents that repeatedly ignored or misread explicit reviewer instructions (PDF p. 4).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Reviewer abandonment was the largest category (228 of 562, 38%): PRs closed with no meaningful human interaction. The authors take this as a sign of poor project coordination, and one implication is that agent output needs a named human reviewer who commits to responding (PDF pp. 2, 4). This implication is an interpretation and not a tested result.</li>\n<li>The authors frame failures as socio-technical, arising from misalignment with repository workflows, developer expectations and project coordination, and not only from technical defects (PDF pp. 2, 4).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is an arXiv preprint. The dataset covers open-source projects with more than 100 stars and five specific agents, and may not transfer to private client repositories or to the current versions of these agents.</li>\n<li>Merge status is a proxy for quality. A PR may be closed for reasons unrelated to its content, and the study cannot say how many human-authored PRs fail for the same reasons, since there is no human baseline in this paper.</li>\n<li>The qualitative coding used two annotators with moderate agreement after round one (kappa 0.55) and strong agreement after refinement (0.91), applied to a sample of 100 PRs. The remaining 500 were coded independently, one annotator per PR, as the text describes (PDF p. 2).</li>\n<li>38 sampled PRs were no longer accessible. Table 1 values for review-related variables were not statistically significant in the regression (PDF p. 3). The text read is short and gives few figures beyond those cited.</li>\n<li>The paper does not examine security, data protection or research settings.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-24"]},{"id":"arXiv_2026-02-01_ai-impact-skill-formation","title":"How AI Impacts Skill Formation (preprint, v2)","publisher":"Anthropic Fellows Program, via arXiv","date":"2026-02-01","year":"2026","category":"Empirische Studien","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.20245","binding":"Befund (empirical finding)","dateNote":"1 February 2026 (arXiv stamp; the title page states February 3, 2026, PDF p. 1)","note":"","abstract":"The authors ran a pre-registered, between-subjects randomised experiment on learning a new Python library (Trio, asynchronous programming). 52 participants with more than one year of Python experience did two tasks, 26 with a chat-based AI assistant (GPT-4o) and 26 without, followed by a quiz without AI on debugging, code reading and conceptual understanding (PDF pp. 6-7, 9).","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>The authors ran a pre-registered, between-subjects randomised experiment on learning a new Python library (Trio, asynchronous programming). 52 participants with more than one year of Python experience did two tasks, 26 with a chat-based AI assistant (GPT-4o) and 26 without, followed by a quiz without AI on debugging, code reading and conceptual understanding (PDF pp. 6-7, 9). The AI group scored about 17% lower on the quiz (Cohen's d = 0.738, p = 0.010), with no statistically significant gain in completion time (PDF pp. 2, 9-10). Screen recordings of 51 participants were coded into six AI interaction patterns, of which three preserved learning (PDF pp. 12-14).</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The authors speak of professional skill acquisition in general, motivated by supervising AI. They state that AI-enhanced productivity \"is not a shortcut to competence\" and should be adopted carefully to preserve skill formation, especially in safety-critical domains (PDF p. 1, abstract). The experiment covers programming only, so transfer to research skills such as literature work or statistics is an extrapolation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Using AI impaired conceptual understanding, code reading and debugging. The largest gap was in debugging questions and the smallest in code reading (PDF p. 11). The authors point out that debugging and code reading are the skills needed to verify AI-written code (PDF p. 6).</li>\n<li>Learning-preserving patterns were conceptual inquiry only (n=7, 65% quiz score), generation followed by comprehension questions (n=2, 86%) and hybrid queries asking for code together with explanation (n=3, 68%) (PDF pp. 13-14).</li>\n<li>Low-scoring patterns were full delegation (n=4, 39%), progressive reliance (n=4, 35%) and iterative AI debugging (n=4, 24%). Delegation was the fastest at 19.5 minutes against 23 for the control group, and about 20% of the AI group used it (PDF pp. 13, 19).</li>\n<li>Participants who worked without AI met more errors (median 3 against 1) and resolved them themselves. The authors attribute part of the learning difference to this, which is an interpretation based on observation (PDF pp. 17-18).</li>\n<li>Speed gains were not significant on average. The authors explain this by time spent composing queries, and some participants spent up to 11 minutes on that (PDF pp. 9, 12, 14).</li>\n<li>The authors expect the effect to be at least as large with agentic tools, since these need even less human participation. This is an expectation, since the study used a chat assistant only (PDF p. 19).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The paper says junior staff under time pressure may delegate to AI at the expense of skill development, and that companies shifting to AI-written code with human supervision may end up with humans unable to validate and debug it (PDF p. 19).</li>\n<li>The authors mention that learning or explanatory modes offered by AI services can support cognitive effort (PDF p. 20). A rule on when to use them is not derived in the paper.</li>\n<li>The study gives no tested organisational intervention. Suggestions such as requiring explanation alongside generation are inferences from the interaction patterns, not experimental results.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Authors are affiliated with an AI vendor (Anthropic Fellows Program and Anthropic), and the study used a competitor's model (GPT-4o) in a chat setting. The affiliation is stated on PDF p. 1.</li>\n<li>It is a preprint (version 2). The sample is small (n=52, qualitative groups of 2 to 7 people), the task lasted about one hour, and skill formation was measured by a quiz taken immediately afterwards, not over months (PDF p. 19).</li>\n<li>Participants were crowd workers paid a flat fee, with limited incentive to learn the library, and were instructed to finish as fast as possible (PDF pp. 9, 19).</li>\n<li>Pilot studies on another platform showed non-compliance with the no-AI condition (35% in one pilot). The main study used a platform with screen recording to verify compliance (PDF p. 8).</li>\n<li>Concept-area breakdowns and the six patterns come from exploratory, not pre-registered, analysis (PDF p. 11). The study has no human-tutor comparison and measures neither prompting skill nor long-run outcomes (PDF p. 19).</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-25"]},{"id":"arXiv_2026-09-29_agentic-reproduction-social-science","title":"Read the Paper, Write the Code: Agentic Reproduction of Social-Science Results (preprint, v2)","publisher":"ETH Zurich, TU Wien and University of Basel, via arXiv","date":"2026-09-29","year":"2026","category":"Empirische Studien","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21965","binding":"Befund (empirical finding)","dateNote":"29 September 2026 (arXiv stamp of version 2, PDF p. 1)","note":"","abstract":"The authors built a pipeline in which an agent re-implements the analyses of an empirical paper from a structured, number-free methods description plus the original data, without seeing the paper, the results or the original code.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>The authors built a pipeline in which an agent re-implements the analyses of an empirical paper from a structured, number-free methods description plus the original data, without seeing the paper, the results or the original code. Outputs are compared cell by cell with the published tables, and an LLM-based step traces discrepancies to root causes (PDF pp. 1, 4-7). They tested four LLMs in four agent scaffolds (seven combinations) on 48 economics and political science papers that the Institute for Replication had verified as reproducible, with 222 tables and 14,214 cells (PDF pp. 7-8). Agents largely recovered published results. Coefficient signs agreed 85-91% of the time for the three best agents, and the best agent was within 1.96 original standard errors for over 80% of coefficients. Performance varied strongly by model, scaffold and paper (PDF p. 2). The paper is a preprint.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Strict information isolation was used to keep agents from copying results. Agents worked in a workspace with only the task, the methods description, output templates and a data link, and were prohibited from reading the original package or fetching the paper through the web (PDF p. 6).</li>\n<li>Compliance with the isolation was checked by two audits, a deterministic scan of accessed paths and URLs and an LLM review of the run trace, with suspicious runs inspected manually and rerun on leakage. A separate hardcoding audit looked for statistical results written as numeric literals without a computation path from the data (PDF p. 6; Appendix B.3 and B.4, PDF pp. 45-46). The LLM review was described as over-inclusive (PDF pp. 45-46).</li>\n<li>Comparison was deterministic, by sign agreement, percentage difference and a letter grade per value, and avoided LLM judges for the scoring (PDF p. 6).</li>\n<li>Most failures traced to the papers (original errors or missing data) and, to a lesser extent, to agents. Higher-performing agents showed fewer human-attributed discrepancies, because they more often guessed the unstated choice correctly. The authors read underspecified methods as a main bottleneck for automated reproduction (PDF pp. 13-14, 16).</li>\n<li>The authors argue that code, not the paper text, is the authoritative record of what was done, and propose explicit alignment between narrative and code. They suggest automated reproduction as a diagnostic that exposes underspecification. This is the authors' interpretation (PDF pp. 16-17).</li>\n<li>The authors state that agents taking on more of the research process would require new criteria for scientific validity, including the reliability of conclusions generated without direct human oversight (PDF p. 17).</li>\n<li>Results were not fully stable. More than 80% of tables had a grade spread of at most one step across three runs, but 25-32% of matched coefficients differed between runs by more than 1.96 original standard errors, so fine comparisons between agents need caution (PDF p. 15).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Agent and scaffold choice mattered. GPT-5.4 in OpenCode was best, and the same model in the Codex CLI or mini-SWE-Agent scored worse. The best setup also used far more tokens, time and cost, so the authors say accuracy differences may partly reflect compute budgets (PDF pp. 2, 11-12).</li>\n<li>Agents produced one Python script per table, run in a sandbox, with traces and workspace contents kept for audit (PDF pp. 4, 6, 45).</li>\n<li>Reported scaling and unit problems (dollars against cents) produced many large deviations and were not accounted for by the comparison (PDF p. 10).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a preprint and the authors' own evaluation of their pipeline. The sample is 48 papers already verified as reproducible with data availability, from well-regarded journals, with original code in Stata (56.3%) and R (27.1%) and none in Python (PDF p. 7). Results may not transfer to unverified or poorly documented work.</li>\n<li>The study covers reproduction on the same data. It does not cover replication with new data, robustness analysis, or research without data access (PDF pp. 2, 17).</li>\n<li>Table extraction and the root-cause attribution rely on LLMs. A human audit confirmed 49 of 53 flagged divergences (92%) but only 33 of 49 error-source labels (67%), with 10 judged unsure, so attribution shares are indicative only (PDF p. 13). About 8% of tables were dropped due to extraction issues (PDF p. 5).</li>\n<li>Leakage was probed with a small test of ten Economic Journal papers before and after the models' knowledge cutoff, with no significant difference. The authors say this does not rule out leakage, including from post-training (PDF p. 16).</li>\n<li>Figure-level comparisons were left out of the core analysis because reliable vision-language checking is uncertain (PDF p. 5). Models and scaffolds named in the text are specific versions from 2026.</li>\n<li>Appendix pages beyond those cited (additional tables and prompts) were skimmed and not read in full.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-17"]},{"id":"arXiv_2026-09-29_agents-md-context-files","title":"Evaluating AGENTS.md: Are Repository-Level Context Files Helpful for Coding Agents? (preprint, v3)","publisher":"ETH Zurich and LogicStar.ai, via arXiv","date":"2026-09-29","year":"2026","category":"Empirische Studien","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.11988","binding":"Befund (empirical finding)","dateNote":"29 September 2026 (arXiv stamp of version 3, PDF p. 1)","note":"","abstract":"The study tests whether repository context files such as AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md help coding agents resolve real tasks.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>The study tests whether repository context files such as AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md help coding agents resolve real tasks. It uses 300 SWE-bench Lite tasks from 11 popular Python repositories with LLM-generated context files, and a new benchmark, CtxBench, of 138 instances from 12 niche Python repositories that contain developer-written context files (PDF pp. 2, 5). Four agent and model pairs were tested (Claude Code with Sonnet 4.5, Codex with GPT-5.2 and GPT-5.1 Mini, Qwen Code with Qwen3-30B) in settings with no file, an LLM-generated file and a developer-written file, each sampled once (PDF p. 5). The result is that context files did not significantly change the success rate but raised cost and steps, by about 20% on average for LLM-generated files (PDF p. 6).</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>LLM-generated context files lowered resolution rates in 5 of 8 settings, by 0.5% on SWE-bench and 2% on CtxBench on average, and the changes were not significant (p = 0.87 and 0.37). They raised cost by 20% and 23% and added steps (PDF p. 6).</li>\n<li>Developer-written files improved performance by 2.4% on average (p = 0.21, not significant). They outperformed LLM-generated files with p = 0.038, and raised cost by up to 19% (PDF p. 6). The introduction states a 7% margin for developer-written over LLM-generated files, which does not match these figures directly, and the difference could not be reconciled from the text read (PDF p. 2).</li>\n<li>Agents follow instructions in context files. Named tools were used 1.6 times per instance (uv) and 2.5 times (repository-specific tools) when mentioned, against almost never when not, and context files led to more testing, searching and file reading (PDF p. 8). The authors link the extra cost to this obedience and to additional reasoning tokens (PDF p. 8).</li>\n<li>Repository overviews did not help agents reach the relevant files faster (PDF p. 7).</li>\n<li>The authors conclude that context files should be omitted when LLM-generated, and that human-written ones should contain only instructions missing from the README, such as specific conventions or non-functional requirements, and be evaluated before adoption (PDF p. 2). This is the authors' recommendation, resting on the benchmark results.</li>\n<li>When all documentation files were removed from the repository, LLM-generated context files improved success by 2.7% on average and beat developer-written ones. The authors read this as redundancy with existing documentation (PDF p. 16).</li>\n<li>Stronger models did not write better context files, and the prompt used to generate the files made no consistent difference (PDF pp. 8-9). File length showed no clear effect (PDF p. 16).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field. A general point is that the authors treat agent-developer recommendations as claims to be tested, and say any attempt to improve performance should be rigorously evaluated before deployment (PDF p. 1, abstract).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a preprint (version 3). Authors include employees of LogicStar.ai, a company active in agent tooling, and the text does not discuss this.</li>\n<li>Only Python repositories were studied. The authors note that models may already know Python tooling, which could reduce the value of context files (PDF p. 9).</li>\n<li>Success is measured by passing tests only. Code quality, efficiency and security are not measured, and the authors name them as open (PDF p. 9).</li>\n<li>CtxBench relies on LLM agents to build environments, task descriptions and tests, with manual review of the tests and inspection of all instances (PDF pp. 4-5). Each agent was sampled once, with confidence reported through significance tests (PDF pp. 5-6).</li>\n<li>Results are not statistically significant for most success-rate comparisons, so the claim is \"no demonstrated improvement\" and not \"harm\".</li>\n<li>The study does not address rules files used for safety, compliance or style enforcement, since it measures only task resolution.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[]},{"id":"DFG_2026-03_leitlinie-ki-in-der-begutachtung","title":"Leitlinie zur Nutzung von Künstlicher Intelligenz in der Begutachtung (DFG-Vordruck 4.04)","publisher":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG)","date":"2026-03","year":"2026","category":"Förderer und Forschungsintegrität","url":"https://www.dfg.de/resource/blob/387664/4-04-de.pdf","binding":"Vorgabe (funder requirement for reviewers; the text says AI use is \"ausschließlich entlang der im Weiteren näher ausgeführten Prinzipien zulässig\")","dateNote":"03/2026 (form number \"DFG-Vordruck 4.04 - 03/26\")","note":"","abstract":"The four-page DFG guideline sets the conditions under which reviewers of DFG proposals may use AI systems.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>The four-page DFG guideline sets the conditions under which reviewers of DFG proposals may use AI systems. AI may only support the review, never replace the reviewer's own judgement. Four principles apply: confidentiality, transparency, quality assurance and responsibility.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<p>The source says nothing specific on this field.</p>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>AI is permitted only in a supporting sense; all substantive positions on the funding recommendation rest with the reviewer (PDF p. 2).</li>\n<li>Permitted examples are searching relevant literature, turning the reviewer's own notes into running text where the direction is already clear, and suggestions for structure or language of own content (PDF p. 2).</li>\n<li>Not permitted is generating significant parts of the review automatically and unreflectively, without own expert check (PDF p. 2).</li>\n<li>Confidentiality: proposal content may only be fed into an AI system if the provider does not store it permanently or beyond the concrete purpose (PDF p. 2). One of three conditions must hold: the system runs locally with no cloud transfer, or it is hosted by a trustworthy institution such as a university, or a cloud system is used under a licence agreement that guarantees data security, excludes use of inputs for training, and has the settings actively set, for example \"kein logging\" (PDF pp. 2 to 3).</li>\n<li>Cloud systems are named by example (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini) and are allowed only under those contractual and configuration conditions (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>Systems based on documented, publicly available models with inspectable training data are recommended (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>Transparency: use of AI in a review must be disclosed with the specific purposes and extent, in a few sentences; marking individual passages is not required. \"Eingesetzte KI, die sich nicht auf den (wissenschaftlichen) Inhalt des Gutachtens auswirkt\" need not be disclosed, for example grammar, spell checking and translation (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>Quality assurance: uncritical adoption of AI-generated text is not permitted. Text must be checked for correctness, currency and bias, with explicit mention of status-quo bias and automation bias (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>Responsibility lies fully with the reviewer and cannot be transferred to an AI system (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Passing on proposal content without authorisation, possibly by feeding it into LLMs that use inputs as training data, can breach confidentiality and constitute scientific misconduct under § 9 Nr. 2 VerfOwF (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule for DHCraft when it acts as reviewer for a DFG procedure: no proposal text goes into any AI system unless one of the three confidentiality conditions is verified, and AI use is disclosed with purpose and extent.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<p>The source says nothing specific on this field.</p>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The guideline justifies itself by reference to the AI Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, the GDPR and copyright law (PDF p. 1). It anchors its principles in DFG code Leitlinie 16 on confidential treatment of material under review (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Candidate organisational rule: a register of approved AI services with their contractual training exclusion and logging settings, usable as evidence of the confidentiality condition.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Addressed to reviewers of DFG proposals only, not to applicants, and not to FWF or other funders.</li>\n<li>Does not say how a trustworthy hosting institution is to be assessed or how \"kein logging\" settings are verified.</li>\n<li>Does not cover agentic tools, tool use or code; it speaks of AI systems in general.</li>\n<li>The text names the legal grounds only in passing and does not interpret them.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-5","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-16"]},{"id":"DSB-AT_2026_newsletter-1-2026-ki-und-datenschutz","title":"Newsletter Nr. 1/2026, article Updates zu KI und Datenschutz","publisher":"Österreichische Datenschutzbehörde (DSB)","date":"2026 (issue 1/2026)","year":"2026","category":"Datenschutz","url":"https://dsb.gv.at/sites/site0344/media/downloads/nl0126bf.pdf","binding":"Empfehlung (non-binding authority information; it restates binding law but is itself a newsletter)","dateNote":"2026 (issue Nr. 1/2026; no day stated on the source; content refers to events up to 15 April 2026 and a role change on 1 April 2026)","note":"","abstract":"A 15-page authority newsletter whose relevant part is a short article by a DSB department head (PDF pp. 3 to 5) on data protection and the AI Regulation when using AI tools.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>A 15-page authority newsletter whose relevant part is a short article by a DSB department head (PDF pp. 3 to 5) on data protection and the AI Regulation when using AI tools. Its main position is that the GDPR applies in parallel to the AI Regulation and is not displaced by it, and that the AI Regulation is not a legal basis under Art. 6 GDPR. The remaining pages (decisions, court rulings, opinions on bills) are unrelated to AI.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The GDPR applies whenever personal data is processed at least partly with automated support, including AI systems, and is \"technologieneutral\" (PDF p. 3). \"Die DSGVO gilt parallel zur KI-VO und wird von ihr nicht verdrängt\" (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>For every processing, also with AI tools, the Art. 5 principles apply: transparency, purpose limitation, data minimisation, storage limitation, integrity and confidentiality. Controllers carry the burden of proof (Art. 5 para. 2) (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>Every processing needs one of the lawfulness grounds in Art. 6 para. 1 GDPR across the whole life cycle of the AI system. \"Die KI-VO ist grundsätzlich keine Rechtsgrundlage iSd Art. 6 DSGVO\" (PDF pp. 3 to 4).</li>\n<li>Data subject rights (access, rectification, erasure) must be preserved when AI systems are used, notably the right not to be subject to a solely automated individual decision including profiling (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>A data protection impact assessment under Art. 35 GDPR is described as \"erforderlich und hilfreich\" for AI systems whose effects on data subjects are hard to assess, and may be combined with the fundamental rights impact assessment under the AI Regulation (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: before an agent touches client personal data, name the Art. 6 ground, document the Art. 5 principles and check whether a DPIA is needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<p>The source says nothing specific on this field.</p>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<p>The source says nothing specific on this field. It notes only that the technology must be examined where it affects storage duration or the exercise of data subject rights (PDF p. 3).</p>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The AI Regulation classifies AI systems by risk, subjects high-risk systems to requirements and conformity assessment, prohibits some practices, and regulates some systems hardly at all beyond transparency duties (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Austria's national implementation of the AI Regulation, including designation of competent authorities, is still outstanding; the DSB is to be sectoral market surveillance authority for certain high-risk systems under Art. 74 para. 8 and was notified as fundamental rights authority under Art. 77 (PDF pp. 1 to 2, 4).</li>\n<li>The article remarks that autonomous AI agents \"werden es möglicherweise bald sein\" (ubiquitous) and that the DSB receives more complaints concerning AI systems (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>The Digital Omnibus proposals of November 2025 (a data, cybersecurity and privacy omnibus and an AI omnibus) were still under negotiation by the co-legislators; the AI omnibus foresees a possible postponement of high-risk obligations (PDF pp. 4 to 5). Outcome unknown at the time of writing.</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: keep a register of AI systems used and their risk class, and track the Omnibus outcome and the Austrian implementation law.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A newsletter article of a few paragraphs; no interpretation of the AI Regulation or GDPR beyond restating provisions, and no mention of agentic systems beyond one sentence.</li>\n<li>Addressed to the general public in Austria, not specifically to providers or small companies.</li>\n<li>The Omnibus status is a snapshot; the proposals were later adopted in some form (see the Official Journal source in this knowledge base), so this section is outdated on that point.</li>\n<li>The date is only given as year and issue number.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-4"]},{"id":"EDPB_2026-02-11_work-programme-2026-2027","title":"EDPB Work Programme 2026-2027","publisher":"European Data Protection Board (EDPB)","date":"2026-02-11","year":"2026","category":"Datenschutz","url":"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2026-02/edpb_work-programme_2026-2027_en.pdf","binding":"Planning document of the EDPB. It creates no obligations for controllers or processors and states no rule on Art. 37 GDPR.","dateNote":"Adopted on 11 February 2026 (cover page)","note":"","abstract":"The work programme lists the guidance the EDPB intends to develop in 2026 and 2027. Among the planned guidance on key concepts it names a \"Targeted update of the DPO Guidelines\" (PDF p. 3).","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>The work programme lists the guidance the EDPB intends to develop in 2026 and 2027. Among the planned guidance on key concepts it names a \"Targeted update of the DPO Guidelines\" (PDF p. 3). It does not say which points the update covers or when it will appear. It also lists joint guidelines on the interplay between the AI Act and the GDPR (PDF p. 7) and guidelines on generative AI and data scraping (PDF p. 8).</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<p>The document answers none of the questions on the duty to designate a data protection officer. Its only use is to show that the existing DPO guidance is under revision and that AI related guidance is announced.</p>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The existing DPO guidelines remain the reference for the criteria of Art. 37 GDPR until the announced update is published, so a decision on designation should be revisited when it appears. This is an inference from the planning entry. Location: PDF p. 3, \"Targeted update of the DPO Guidelines\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Guidelines on the processing of data for scientific research purposes are on the same list, which matters for research projects with personal data. Location: PDF p. 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Joint guidelines on the AI Act and GDPR interplay and guidelines on generative AI data scraping are announced, but not yet content. Location: PDF p. 7 and PDF p. 8.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>DHCraft should record the designation decision with its reasons and plan a review when the updated DPO guidelines are published. This is an inference, not a statement of the source. Location: PDF p. 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<p>The document is a plan and states no criteria, no small company rule and no AI specific rule on DPOs. The content and timing of the update are not given. The list is subject to change by the EDPB.</p>","rules":[]},{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","title":"Living guidelines on the responsible use of generative AI in research, fourth version","publisher":"European Commission, DG Research and Innovation","date":"2026-10-15","year":"2026","category":"Förderer und Forschungsintegrität","url":"https://research-and-innovation.ec.europa.eu/document/download/2b6cf7e5-36ac-41cb-aab5-0d32050143dc_en?filename=ec_rtd_ai-guidelines.pdf","binding":"Empfehlung (non-binding guidance)","dateNote":"October 2026; the fourth version is stated as published on 15 October 2026 (PDF p. 18); next revision foreseen in Q3 2027","note":"","abstract":"A 24-page non-binding guidance document with recommendations for researchers, research organisations and research funding organisations on generative AI, extended in this version to agentic AI.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>A 24-page non-binding guidance document with recommendations for researchers, research organisations and research funding organisations on generative AI, extended in this version to agentic AI. Four principles frame it: consider whether AI is the right tool, accountability stays with researchers and their institutions, confidentiality is non-negotiable, and transparency and disclosure build trust. It translates existing research integrity expectations and states that it creates no new standards (PDF p. 6).</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Third parties may use generative AI in meetings, note-taking or summaries of confidential documents. Researchers and organisations should inform third parties about such tools and risks, and tell them when they themselves use AI tools and how collected information is protected (PDF p. 10).</li>\n<li>Researchers should not provide third parties' personal data to external generative AI systems unless a lawful basis such as consent exists and a clear purpose is defined; a footnote adds that bases other than consent are possible (PDF p. 8).</li>\n<li>\"The use of AI agents may never be considered a delegation of control or responsibility\" (PDF p. 7).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: tell clients which AI tools and agents touch their material, and do not put client personal data into external systems without a lawful basis and a defined purpose.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Responsibility: researchers are accountable for the integrity of AI-supported content. AI systems are neither authors, co-authors nor owners (PDF p. 7).</li>\n<li>Agents: when tasks are implemented autonomously, researchers define check points so that critical decisions, for example methodological ones, are validated by them (PDF p. 7). \"AI agents must be deployed strictly as advanced research tools, not independent scientists.\" Hypotheses, processed data and conclusions of an agent need human validation (PDF p. 14).</li>\n<li>Disclosure: tools used substantially are disclosed with what they contributed, following the rules of discipline, journal, funder or institution. Examples of substantial use are using AI as a method or source, interpreting data analysis, literature review, identifying research gaps, formulating aims, developing hypotheses; basic editorial support is not substantial (PDF p. 7, footnote 10). Spell checking, grammar and formatting generally need no disclosure; \"in case of doubt, disclosure is advised\" (PDF p. 10).</li>\n<li>Oversight and verification strategies, such as measures against hallucination and bias, should be described so readers can assess validity (PDF p. 7).</li>\n<li>Reproducibility: because generative and agentic AI is non-deterministic, authors decide whether prompts, logs, model versions and parameters should be retained or shared, especially when agents perform sequences of research tasks or contribute to methodological decisions (PDF p. 7).</li>\n<li>Confidentiality: do not upload unpublished or sensitive work into external AI systems unless there are assurances it will not be re-used, for example for training (PDF pp. 7 to 8). Check institutional guidelines, privacy options, who owns the tool and where it runs (PDF p. 8).</li>\n<li>Agents with access to sensitive information: after a suitability assessment the researcher instructs and constrains potential confidentiality breaches and remains accountable for the agent's use (PDF p. 8). Researchers must ensure agents do not breach legislation and are not exempt from legal responsibility because an agent acted on their behalf (PDF p. 8).</li>\n<li>Plagiarism and citations: outputs may rely on others' results and need recognition; the researcher keeps final responsibility for citation correctness (PDF p. 8). Invented citations and wrong summaries even of existing papers: researchers check all references and the content of citations and summaries (PDF p. 9).</li>\n<li>Peer review and proposal evaluation: comply with the applicable rules, never delegate the judgement, use AI only for supporting tasks, do not disclose unpublished proposals, manuscripts or ideas through non-secured tools, prefer tools provided by the institution, funder or publisher, and disclose substantive use (PDF p. 9). AI cannot be used to draft evaluation reports or assess scientific merit (PDF p. 9, footnote 16).</li>\n<li>Applicants: funders are advised to ask applicants to declare substantial AI use and the role of AI in the proposed research (PDF p. 13).</li>\n<li>Candidate rules: a disclosure statement per output type; retain prompts, logs and model versions for agent runs that influence methodology; every agent-produced citation verified against the source.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Coding agents are given as an example of agentic AI (PDF p. 5). Agentic systems are characterised by goal complexity, environmental complexity, adaptability and independent execution, and typically use generative models as reasoning core in a \"harness = model + infrastructure\" architecture (PDF p. 14).</li>\n<li>The source gives no technical rules for code. Relevant by extension are the points on check points, logging of agent runs, plagiarism in code outputs (PDF p. 8) and prevention of legal breaches by agents.</li>\n<li>A footnote mentions sustainable coding as part of reducing the environmental impact of AI use (PDF p. 8, footnote 15).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Organisations should train staff in verifying output, privacy, bias and intellectual property, support compliance, and foster an atmosphere where disclosure carries no disadvantage (PDF p. 11).</li>\n<li>Keep track of what generative AI is used for within the organisation (PDF p. 11).</li>\n<li>Whenever possible and necessary, implement self-governed locally hosted or cloud-based tools, or tools governed by trustworthy third parties meeting EU data protection and research security requirements, and ensure appropriate cybersecurity, especially for internet-connected systems (PDF pp. 11 to 12).</li>\n<li>Funders and organisations should account for hidden prompts, meaning invisible instructions embedded in received documents, and set rules and safeguards in IT systems (PDF p. 13). This is stated for funders processing applications, but the risk applies to any agent that reads third-party documents.</li>\n<li>Human oversight models are explained as human in the loop, on the loop and in command (PDF p. 19, footnote 29).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: a register of approved AI tools with hosting location and data re-use terms, mandatory training, and treatment of third-party documents as possible carriers of hidden instructions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-binding; legislation and funder or institutional requirements prevail (PDF p. 6).</li>\n<li>Addressed to researchers, research organisations and funders; it does not address commercial service providers such as DHCraft, and gives no technical detail on agent security.</li>\n<li>Disclosure thresholds are explicitly described as an open question; a global reporting standard is being developed at the World Conference on Research Integrity 2026 (PDF pp. 14 to 15).</li>\n<li>Footnote 18 notes that disclosure of AI assistance may lead to lower assessment scores in studies (PDF p. 11); the source reports this as a concern, not as a rule.</li>\n<li>Living document; the next revision is foreseen for Q3 2027 (PDF p. 18).</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-7","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-10","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-11","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-12","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-13","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-14","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-16","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-19","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-29"]},{"id":"EU-Kommission_2026-07-20_guidelines-article-50-transparency","title":"Guidelines on the implementation of the transparency obligations for certain AI systems under Article 50 of Regulation (EU) 2024/1689","publisher":"European Commission","date":"2026-07-20","year":"2026","category":"Recht und Regulierung","url":"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/guidelines-transparency-obligations-providers-and-deployers-ai-systems","binding":"Empfehlung (non-binding guidance on a binding law; the text says \"These Guidelines are non-binding\", PDF p. 4)","dateNote":"Brussels, 20.7.2026 (as stated on the source)","note":"","abstract":"A 51-page guidance document on Article 50 of the AI Act. It explains the four transparency duties: information that one interacts with an AI system (Art. 50(1)), machine-readable marking and detection of synthetic content (Art. 50(2)), information about emotion recognition and biometric categorisation (Art. 50(3)), and labelling of deep fakes and of AI-generated texts on matters of public interest (Art. 50(4)).","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>A 51-page guidance document on Article 50 of the AI Act. It explains the four transparency duties: information that one interacts with an AI system (Art. 50(1)), machine-readable marking and detection of synthetic content (Art. 50(2)), information about emotion recognition and biometric categorisation (Art. 50(3)), and labelling of deep fakes and of AI-generated texts on matters of public interest (Art. 50(4)). It names AI agents and coding agents explicitly and clarifies what falls outside the duties, which is relevant for DHCraft both as a builder of agent workflows and as an author of texts.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Role test for deployers: a legal person remains the deployer even if contractors or freelancers operate the system on its behalf and under its control; its own employees are not separate deployers (PDF p. 7, para. 14). A company that commissions an agency without deciding whether or how AI is used is not a deployer (PDF pp. 7 to 8, para. 14).</li>\n<li>Deployers who use AI to generate or manipulate texts that inform the public on matters of public interest must disclose this (Art. 50(4)), unless the text underwent human review or editorial control and a person holds editorial responsibility (PDF p. 44, paras. 132 to 133). Human review means deliberate examination of the substance by persons with relevant knowledge, with fact-checking as minimum; spell-checking, automated review or cursory approval do not count (PDF p. 44, paras. 134 to 135). Substantive AI changes after the review void the exception (PDF pp. 44 to 45, para. 136). The person or function with editorial responsibility should be named publicly (PDF p. 45, para. 138).</li>\n<li>Explicit examples outside Art. 50(4): \"AI-manipulated text by a consultant for a client advice regarding measures to be taken for regulatory compliance\" (PDF p. 43); also private or organisation-internal correspondence is not \"published\" (PDF p. 43).</li>\n<li>Examples inside Art. 50(4) that would matter for publishing: AI-generated summaries of articles, and AI-manipulated parts of articles on public-interest topics, unless reviewed (PDF p. 43). Translations that underwent human review are given as an example of the exception (PDF p. 45).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: before publishing AI-assisted texts on public-interest topics for or with clients (reports, websites, news-style items), document a substantive human review with fact-check and a named person with editorial responsibility, otherwise label the text.</li>\n<li>Art. 50 is \"without prejudice\" to the GDPR and consumer law; the AI notice does not replace data protection information duties (PDF pp. 20 to 21, paras. 50 to 52). Where a service is AI-driven, the AI function may be an essential characteristic that must be disclosed before a consumer contract (PDF pp. 20 to 21, para. 50).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Exclusion: Art. 2(6) AI Act excludes AI systems and models, including their outputs, developed and put into service for the sole purpose of scientific research and development. This is \"without prejudice to applicable research ethical standards and EU data protection\" law. If the system or outputs are used for other purposes, Art. 50 applies (PDF p. 9, para. 21). Art. 2(8) excludes research, testing and development before placing on the market; testing in real-world conditions is not covered (PDF p. 10, para. 22).</li>\n<li>Texts: AI-generated summaries and paraphrasing beyond minor correction count as semantic changes that need marking by the provider (PDF p. 30). This is a provider duty; for researchers using such tools it matters only if they place their own system on the market.</li>\n<li>Academic peer review is cited as an example of human review in the Art. 50(4) text exception (PDF p. 44, para. 134), and an internally peer-reviewed academic blog as example of editorial responsibility held by a research centre (PDF p. 45).</li>\n<li>Otherwise the source says nothing specific on research integrity or funding applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Coding agents are listed as AI systems \"capable of directly interacting with natural persons\" and therefore covered by Art. 50(1) (PDF p. 13, para. 31). AI agents must be designed so that they disclose their artificial nature and the person on whose behalf they act. Where the provider cannot predict contacts with natural persons, the agent should be designed at architecture level, and instructed, to disclose itself wherever interaction with a person is reasonably likely. Agents should also disclose themselves to the persons instructing them at key steps such as authorisation, reporting and validation, and at every new interaction (PDF p. 13, para. 31).</li>\n<li>Examples of disclosure: an email generated by an AI agent carries an AI label at the top (PDF p. 15, para. 36); AI identifiers and verifiable credentials, with EU digital identity wallets named as one option (PDF p. 15, footnote 21). Terms and conditions or documentation alone are insufficient (PDF p. 16, para. 38).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: any agent that sends mail, messages or comments to people, for example an issue or pull request comment, states that it is an AI agent and on whose behalf it acts.</li>\n<li>Exceptions: AI-powered code assistance and code review chatbots \"available only to professional developers\" fall under the obviousness exception, as do internal assistants for trained, AI-literate staff (PDF p. 18). The exception is read restrictively; where the general public could be reached it cannot be relied on (PDF pp. 17 to 18, para. 45).</li>\n<li>Machine-to-machine calls between AI systems, including agents, whose outputs are not meant to reach natural persons are outside Art. 50(1) (PDF p. 13, para. 30). Text and code auto-completion is listed as outside the interaction duty (PDF p. 14).</li>\n<li>Marking duty (providers only): source code, configuration, SQL, IaC, YAML, JSON, scripts, APIs and libraries are outside the Art. 50(2) marking duty, as are short sequences such as single words or alt-text and exclusively machine-to-machine outputs, including agent-to-agent communication (PDF p. 24, para. 68). Agent actions are in scope only if they output AI-generated audio, image, video or text perceptible by people; chain of thought, web requests and browser actions are not (PDF p. 23, para. 63).</li>\n<li>Modification: a company that modifies an existing generative AI system and puts it into service under its own name becomes a provider of the new system (PDF p. 7).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Dates: Art. 50 applies from 2 August 2026. A transitional rule from the AI Omnibus gives existing generative systems until 2 December 2026 for the marking obligation only; the interaction disclosure applies from 2 August 2026 (PDF p. 51, para. 153). Content created before 2 August 2026 need not be labelled retroactively, but texts created earlier and published on or after that date must be labelled if in scope (PDF p. 51, para. 154).</li>\n<li>Literacy: Art. 4 AI Act applies to providers and deployers within the scope of Art. 50 (PDF p. 11, para. 25). Compliance with Art. 50 does not mean the use is lawful otherwise (PDF p. 10, para. 25).</li>\n<li>Compliance evidence: adherence to a code of practice assessed as adequate is called the most straightforward way to demonstrate compliance. Non-signatories are expected to explain their measures, for instance by a gap analysis against the code, and may face more information requests (PDF pp. 48 to 49, paras. 146 to 148). A code of practice on transparency of AI-generated content was published on 10 June 2026 (PDF p. 48, footnote 43).</li>\n<li>Penalties: up to EUR 15 000 000 or 3 % of worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher; for SMEs whichever is lower (PDF p. 50, para. 152).</li>\n<li>Personal non-professional use is excluded for deployers, but \"freelance activity\" counts as professional (PDF p. 9, para. 19).</li>\n<li>Information must be clear, distinguishable, at the latest at first interaction or exposure, and accessible (PDF pp. 47 to 48, paras. 141 to 144).</li>\n<li>Candidate rules: a register of DHCraft's roles per AI system (provider, deployer), a decision record on whether the exceptions apply, and a calendar entry for the transitional date and for the next revision of the guidelines.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-binding; only the Court of Justice can interpret the AI Act authoritatively (PDF p. 4, para. 5). The document is first-edition guidance and may be amended or withdrawn (PDF p. 51, para. 155).</li>\n<li>The cover page labels the file an annex to a Communication approving the \"draft\" guidelines; whether this is the final published text is not stated in the file.</li>\n<li>Addresses mainly providers of interactive and generative systems and deployers of deep-fake and public-interest text content. It does not address ordinary internal use of coding assistants by developers, research integrity, funding rules or data protection beyond cross-references.</li>\n<li>The sections on deep fakes (PDF pp. 34 to 42), emotion recognition and biometric categorisation (PDF pp. 31 to 34) were read at lower depth since they have no obvious application to DHCraft's work; no rule is derived from them here.</li>\n<li>Page citations refer to PDF pages; the table of contents numbers are printed pages and differ by one.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-8","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-9","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-15","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-23"]},{"id":"EU-Parlament-Rat_2016-04-27_dsgvo-art-37-39","title":"Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR), Recital 97 and Articles 37 to 39 (excerpt)","publisher":"European Parliament and Council","date":"2016-04-27 (exception)","year":"2016","category":"Datenschutz","url":"https://publications.europa.eu/resource/celex/32016R0679","binding":"Pflicht (law)","dateNote":"27 April 2016 (date of the act), published 4 May 2016","note":"Included as an approved exception to the 2026 date window, for the question of designating a data protection officer.","abstract":"Controller and processor must designate a data protection officer where processing is done by a public authority or body, where core activities require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects on a large scale, or where core activities consist of large-scale processing of special categories (Art. 9) or criminal-conviction data (Art. 10) (Art. 37(1)(a) to (c)).","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Controller and processor must designate a data protection officer where processing is done by a public authority or body, where core activities require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects on a large scale, or where core activities consist of large-scale processing of special categories (Art. 9) or criminal-conviction data (Art. 10) (Art. 37(1)(a) to (c)).</li>\n<li>Outside these cases designation is voluntary, unless Union or Member State law requires it (Art. 37(4)).</li>\n<li>The officer may be staff or an external service provider, must be chosen for professional qualities and expert knowledge, and the contact details must be published and communicated to the supervisory authority (Art. 37(5) to (7)).</li>\n<li>The officer must be involved in all data protection issues, receives no instructions on the tasks, reports to the highest management level, and may hold other tasks only without conflict of interests (Art. 38(1), (3), (6)).</li>\n<li>Tasks are to inform and advise, to monitor compliance, to advise on the impact assessment, and to cooperate with and act as contact for the supervisory authority (Art. 39(1)).</li>\n<li>In the private sector, core activities relate to primary activities, not to processing as an ancillary activity (Recital 97).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Where DHCraft acts as processor, Art. 37(1) applies to it directly, so the test is applied to DHCraft's own core activities and not only to the client's (Art. 37(1), \"the controller and the processor\").</li>\n<li>Processing of testimony recordings or correspondence with special-category content triggers the duty only if that processing is a core activity and on a large scale (Art. 37(1)(c)).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Research work for clients that touches special-category data falls under the same test. The text contains no research or archive exemption from Art. 37 (Art. 37(1)).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Agents that process personal data while building software are, on the text, ancillary support rather than a primary activity unless the product itself is that processing (Recital 97, sentence on ancillary activities). This is an inference from the recital.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A voluntary designation is possible, and an external officer under a service contract is allowed (Art. 37(4), (6)).</li>\n<li>A designated officer needs resources, independence and published contact details, which shapes the internal rules for any such role (Art. 37(7), Art. 38(2), (3)).</li>\n<li>Roles that determine purposes and means of processing risk a conflict of interests (Art. 38(6)).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The excerpt contains only Recital 97 and Articles 37 to 39. It does not define \"large scale\", \"core activities\" or \"regular and systematic monitoring\", and Recital 91 and Art. 4 are not included.</li>\n<li>Art. 37(4) leaves room for national law, which is covered by the separate DSG source.</li>\n<li>The text gives no numeric thresholds and no exemption by company size (Art. 37(1)).</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-27"]},{"id":"EU-Parlament-Rat_2026-07-08_verordnung-2026-1744-digital-omnibus-ki","title":"Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 amending Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (Digital Omnibus on AI)","publisher":"European Parliament and Council","date":"2026-07-08 (OJ L of 24.7.2026)","year":"2026","category":"Recht und Regulierung","url":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng","binding":"Pflicht (law, directly applicable in all Member States)","dateNote":"Adopted 8 July 2026, published 24.7.2026; enters into force on the third day after publication (PDF p. 41), which makes 27 July 2026 (derived from the publication date; the delegation dates in PDF p. 34 also refer to 27 July 2026)","note":"","abstract":"The regulation is an amending act. It does not restate the AI Act but changes it in targeted ways: it softens the AI literacy duty, postpones the high-risk obligations, adds two prohibitions, extends SME relief and centralises enforcement for general-purpose-model-based systems at the AI Office.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>The regulation is an amending act. It does not restate the AI Act but changes it in targeted ways: it softens the AI literacy duty, postpones the high-risk obligations, adds two prohibitions, extends SME relief and centralises enforcement for general-purpose-model-based systems at the AI Office. For DHCraft as a small company the practically relevant changes concern AI literacy (Art. 4), the new Art. 5 prohibitions, the dates of application, the Art. 50(2) transitional period and the SME and small mid-cap relief. The text contains no rule specific to AI agents, apart from one code list entry.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Art. 4 AI Act is replaced. Providers and deployers \"shall take measures to support the development of AI literacy\" of their staff and of other persons operating or using AI systems on their behalf, considering knowledge, experience, context and the persons on whom the systems are used. The duty to ensure a sufficient level is dropped: it \"does not require providers or deployers to guarantee any specific level of AI literacy of any individual\" (PDF p. 16, Art. 1 point 5). Recital 8 gives the reason as compliance burden for smaller enterprises (PDF p. 3).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: DHCraft documents measures that build AI literacy for staff and for external persons working with AI systems on its behalf, such as freelancers or client staff in joint projects.</li>\n<li>Art. 2(7) AI Act is restated: Union data protection law applies to personal data processed in connection with the regulation, and the regulation does not affect the GDPR (PDF p. 15). The regulation therefore gives no legal basis for processing client personal data with AI.</li>\n<li>Art. 27(4) and (5): where a deployer must do a fundamental rights impact assessment, it may cross-reference an existing GDPR data protection impact assessment, and the AI Office is to provide a questionnaire template (PDF p. 20). This only matters where DHCraft deploys a high-risk system.</li>\n<li>New Art. 4a allows exceptional processing of special categories of personal data for bias detection and correction, under conditions such as necessity, pseudonymisation, access controls, no transfer to third parties and deletion (PDF p. 17). It extends to providers and deployers of other AI systems and creates no duty to do bias detection (PDF p. 17).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<p>The source says nothing specific on this field. Art. 4a on special categories of personal data for bias detection (PDF p. 17) is the only research-adjacent provision, and only where bias detection is done.</p>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>New prohibitions in Art. 5(1)(ba) and (bb): AI systems that generate or manipulate realistic intimate images, video or audio of an identifiable person without explicit consent, and systems that generate or manipulate child sexual abuse material (PDF p. 18). For providers the ban applies where this is the intended purpose, or where it is a reasonably foreseeable and reproducible outcome and no reasonable and adequate safeguards exist. For deployers it applies where they use the system for that purpose (PDF p. 18, Art. 5(1a)). Recital 12 lists safeguards such as data cleaning, refusal training, runtime guardrails and output filters (PDF p. 5).</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: DHCraft does not build or deploy generative tools that can produce such material, and applies output controls to any image or video generation component it builds.</li>\n<li>Art. 50(2) marking of synthetic audio, image, video or text content: providers of systems placed on the market before 2 August 2026 must comply by 2 December 2026 (PDF p. 35, Art. 111(4); recital 38 on PDF p. 12). The code of practice mechanism in Art. 50(7) is retained, but the Commission may adopt an implementing act with common rules if the code is judged inadequate (PDF p. 22).</li>\n<li>Annex XIV lists AI technology codes for notified bodies, including \"AI systems based on other emerging AI technologies not covered by other codes, including Agentic AI\" (PDF p. 38). This is a classification code for conformity assessment bodies, not an obligation on agent users.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Dates of application: the prohibitions of Art. 5 apply since 2 February 2025, with the new points (ba) and (bb) and Art. 5(1a) and (1b) applying from 2 December 2026 (PDF p. 35, Art. 113(a)). High-risk obligations in Chapter III Sections 1 to 3 apply from 2 December 2027 for Annex III systems and from 2 August 2028 for Annex I systems (PDF p. 35; recital 40 on PDF p. 12).</li>\n<li>Article 111(2) is rephrased: operators of high-risk systems placed on the market before the application date are covered only if the design is significantly changed, and providers and deployers of high-risk systems for public authorities must comply by 2 August 2030 (PDF p. 35).</li>\n<li>SMEs and small mid-cap enterprises receive definitions and relief: simplified technical documentation form (PDF p. 19), proportionate quality management (PDF pp. 19, 26), simplified quality management for all SMEs (PDF p. 26), priority sandbox access (PDF pp. 23 to 24), and a rule that fines for SMCs are the lower of the percentage or the amount (PDF p. 35). Member States must consider SME interests and economic viability when imposing penalties (PDF p. 35).</li>\n<li>Where a high-risk system is modified by a new provider, the initial provider must cooperate and hand over documentation (PDF pp. 19 to 20, Art. 25).</li>\n<li>Candidate rules: classify every AI system DHCraft builds or deploys by AI Act role (provider, deployer) and risk class, record the dates above in the compliance calendar, and track the delegated acts and guidelines due by 2 August 2027 (PDF pp. 16, 34).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>An amending regulation; its articles are only comprehensible together with the consolidated AI Act, which is not in this source.</li>\n<li>Reading the amended Art. 4 against the earlier wording relies on the recitals, which describe the old duty; the old text itself is not included in the source.</li>\n<li>Addresses providers and deployers of AI systems and enforcement authorities. It contains no rule on AI agents as a working method (such as coding agents), on research, funding or peer review.</li>\n<li>Large parts (Arts. 75 to 75d on the AI Office, annexes, amendments to Regulations 2018/1139 and 2023/1230) concern product legislation and enforcement and are of no practical relevance to DHCraft.</li>\n<li>The entry-into-force date is derived from the publication date, because the text only says \"the third day following\" publication.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-26"]},{"id":"FiveEyes_2026-05-01_careful-adoption-agentic-ai","title":"Careful adoption of agentic AI services","publisher":"ASD's ACSC, CISA, NSA, Canadian Centre for Cyber Security, NCSC-NZ, NCSC-UK","date":"2026-05-01","year":"2026","category":"IT-Sicherheit","url":"https://www.ncsc.govt.nz/assets/guidance/Documents/Careful-adoption-of-agentic-AI-services_FINAL.pdf","binding":"Empfehlung (non-binding guidance)","dateNote":"1 May 2026, stated on the NCSC-NZ landing page (https://ncsc.govt.nz/protect-your-organisation/careful-adoption-of-agentic-ai-services/). The PDF itself carries only the year in its copyright line (PDF p. 29).","note":"","abstract":"The document is joint security guidance from five national cyber agencies on LLM-based agentic AI systems.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>The document is joint security guidance from five national cyber agencies on LLM-based agentic AI systems. It describes five groups of risk (privilege, design and configuration, behaviour, structural, accountability) and sets out best practices for designing, developing, deploying and operating agents (PDF pp. 4, 7-23). Its main position is that organisations should fold agentic AI security into their existing cyber security frameworks, adopt agents incrementally, never grant broad or unrestricted access, and use agents only for low-risk and non-sensitive tasks (PDF pp. 4, 7, 25). It is a guidance text with scenario examples and no empirical study.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The authoring agencies recommend never granting agentic AI broad or unrestricted access, especially to sensitive data or critical systems, and using it only for low-risk, non-sensitive tasks (PDF p. 4).</li>\n<li>Agentic systems aggregate sensitive material such as user prompts, organisational data in retrieval stores and API keys, which makes them an attractive target (PDF p. 12).</li>\n<li>Third-party tools and agents are a supply chain risk (look-alike \"squatted\" tools, dynamically loaded packages). Recommended controls are a trusted registry of components, verification of origin and currency, an allow list of approved tools and versions, and a software bill of materials when procuring agentic systems (PDF pp. 11-12, 18).</li>\n<li>Policies should define legal accountability and risk ownership for agentic systems (PDF p. 19).</li>\n<li>The document notes that governance mechanisms designed for human actors do not always transfer to autonomous agents (PDF p. 7).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field.</li>\n<li>General accuracy points apply. LLMs may produce plausible but wrong output, and grounded or tool-enabled agents may still rely on internal knowledge without saying so (PDF p. 13).</li>\n<li>Recommended mitigations are to validate critical aspects of agent output against multiple sources and to require agents to give specific references showing where key parts of a response came from (PDF pp. 17-18, 22).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Apply least privilege and scope each agent narrowly. Use just-in-time, short-lived credentials for privileged actions and replace long-lived static secrets with ephemeral ones (PDF pp. 23, 28).</li>\n<li>Give each agent its own distinct identity, and keep agents from changing their own privileges or starting unapproved delegation (PDF pp. 15, 18).</li>\n<li>Run agents in isolated environments with segmentation, separate high-risk agents into distinct domains, and keep agents in enclaves without write access to logs (PDF p. 20).</li>\n<li>Specify clear objectives with explicit do-not-do rules, and use hard constraints such as deny lists and API-level policies that the agent cannot override (PDF p. 20).</li>\n<li>Human approval should be required for actions where an error is costly, with system resets, network egress and deletion of critical records named as examples. Requests to delete logs or audit records should be quarantined until a human approves them. The decision when approval is needed belongs to designers or operators, not to the agent (PDF p. 22).</li>\n<li>Use versioning and rollback so that a system can return to known-good behaviour, and log tool usage in human-readable form (PDF pp. 17-18).</li>\n<li>Specification gaming is a named risk, for example an agent disabling security updates to maximise uptime (PDF p. 10).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Implement governance policies for autonomous agents, define legal accountability, and build AI literacy in the organisation (PDF p. 19).</li>\n<li>Deploy in phases with graduated autonomy, and expand or roll back scope based on continuous evaluation (PDF p. 19).</li>\n<li>Set fail-safe defaults so that agents stop and escalate in uncertain situations (PDF p. 20).</li>\n<li>Log comprehensively, monitor all agent operations including internal processes, audit regularly for drift in identity and privilege, and combine human review with automated log analysis (PDF pp. 17, 21-22).</li>\n<li>Develop and test incident response procedures for agent compromise, run red teaming against agentic behaviour, and assess regularly whether an agent can bypass its safeguards (PDF pp. 17, 19, 22-23).</li>\n<li>Accountability is hard in agent chains because actions can be opaque, sub-agents can be spawned, and identical prompts can yield different actions, which complicates reproducibility and assurance (PDF pp. 12-13).</li>\n<li>The document states that governance, accountability, monitoring and human oversight are \"not optional safeguards but essential prerequisites\" (PDF p. 25).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It addresses government, critical infrastructure and industry operators, and is written mostly for developers, vendors and operators of agentic systems. Many controls (cryptographic attestation, mutual TLS, trust scoring, centralised policy decision points) presuppose enterprise infrastructure and are not scaled to a small company (PDF pp. 15, 23).</li>\n<li>It is guidance without empirical evidence on effectiveness. Several statements about agent behaviour, such as strategic deception and altered behaviour under evaluation, are asserted without cited studies in the text read (PDF pp. 7, 10).</li>\n<li>It does not address research integrity, funding proposals, peer review or GDPR and EU AI Act obligations. The disclaimer states that it is general in nature and not legal advice (PDF p. 29).</li>\n<li>The publication date is not printed in the PDF itself. It rests on the landing page of NCSC-NZ, see quellen.md.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-6","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-19","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-21","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-22","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-29","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-30","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-31"]},{"id":"FWF_2026-07-07_ki-in-foerderantraegen","title":"AI in Funding Applications: Transparency and Accountability","publisher":"FWF Austrian Science Fund","date":"2026-07-07","year":"2026","category":"Förderer und Forschungsintegrität","url":"https://www.fwf.ac.at/en/news/detail/ai-in-funding-applications-transparency-and-accountability","binding":"Vorgabe (funder requirement)","dateNote":"July 7, 2026 (as stated on the source)","note":"","abstract":"This is a news item of the FWF on the use of AI in funding applications. AI use is generally permitted, but applicants remain solely responsible for the content, significant AI contribution to the design or drafting must be disclosed, and AI errors count as a violation of good scientific practice that the FWF sanctions.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>This is a news item of the FWF on the use of AI in funding applications. AI use is generally permitted, but applicants remain solely responsible for the content, significant AI contribution to the design or drafting must be disclosed, and AI errors count as a violation of good scientific practice that the FWF sanctions.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<p>The source says nothing specific on this field. It applies where DHCraft prepares an FWF application for a client or a partner institution, because the applicant is always responsible for the content (section \"Researchers bear sole responsibility for content\").</p>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>AI use in preparing proposals is generally permitted (section \"AI in Funding Applications\"). \"The use of artificial intelligence in the preparation of research proposals is generally permitted.\"</li>\n<li>Disclosure is required if AI tools contributed significantly to the design or drafting of a proposal; it must be explicit (lead paragraph and first section).</li>\n<li>Disclosure is not required for actions irrelevant to the content, named as literature reviews, language editing and translations (first section).</li>\n<li>The applicant is responsible for everything the application contains, regardless of the extent of AI use, and must check all information for accuracy (section \"Researchers bear sole responsibility for content\").</li>\n<li>Hallucinations, erroneous passages and inaccurate references attributable to AI have been received and count as a violation of the guidelines for good scientific practice. Sanctions can include an application ban or a demand for repayment of funding (section \"Penalties for improper use of AI\").</li>\n<li>Candidate rule: every reference and factual claim in an AI-assisted application is verified by a human against the source before submission, and significant AI contribution is disclosed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<p>The source says nothing specific on this field.</p>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The FWF states that it monitors technical and legal developments and will adjust its regulations as needed (last section). DHCraft should treat the rule as changeable and re-check the FWF page before each submission.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A news item, not the formal guideline text. It does not define \"significant\", nor how or where disclosure is to be made.</li>\n<li>It covers applicants only, not peer review, and does not mention data protection, confidentiality or tool choice.</li>\n<li>The listed sanctions are named as possible (\"can include\"); the source gives no procedure or criteria.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-10","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-11"]},{"id":"NCSC-UK_2026-08-20_managing-cyber-risk-agentic-ai","title":"Managing the cyber risk of agentic AI","publisher":"National Cyber Security Centre (UK)","date":"2026-08-20","year":"2026","category":"IT-Sicherheit","url":"https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blogs/managing-the-cyber-risk-of-agentic-ai","binding":"Empfehlung (non-binding guidance)","dateNote":"20 August 2026 (as stated at the foot of the page)","note":"","abstract":"The post offers interim practical advice for designers and operators of environments in which AI agents act with significant autonomy.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<p>The post offers interim practical advice for designers and operators of environments in which AI agents act with significant autonomy. The NCSC says the advice rests on its own research to date and will be superseded by formal guidance (section \"Advice and effective practice will continue to evolve\"). Its main position is that controls should be proportionate to the autonomy granted, that model-level safeguards are a baseline only, and that sandboxing, observability, attribution and an emergency shutdown are needed in addition to careful prompting and human oversight.</p>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Apply controls in proportion to the autonomy of each deployment. Agents that only suggest or perform tightly constrained low-risk tasks need less than agents that act on production systems (section \"Assess how much autonomy is needed\").</li>\n<li>Restrict the agent's credentials, data and network reach to what the task needs, which limits the \"blast radius\" if the agent malfunctions or is compromised (section \"Control the AI agent's environment\", part b).</li>\n<li>Credentials include API keys, OAuth grants, SSH keys and authenticated sessions. Give an agent its own identity, minimal permissions and short-lived credentials, and where possible inject credentials through a proxy so the agent never holds them (part e).</li>\n<li>If an agent contacts third-party systems, make its traffic identifiable, for example through IP addresses that support reverse lookup or identifying HTTP headers. Treat abuse reports about the agent like vulnerability or abuse reports and follow an incident playbook (section \"Make your AI activity easy to attribute\").</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>The source says nothing specific on this field.</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Always run agents in a sandbox. For high-risk work the most robust option is an isolated, disconnected environment with pre-downloaded tools (part a).</li>\n<li>Deny inbound and outbound traffic by default and allow only what is required. The post gives a four-level maturity model for network access, from unrestricted (level 1) to no external access with a locally hosted model (level 4) (part c).</li>\n<li>It gives a four-level maturity model for compute isolation, from none (level 1) through containers (level 2) and virtualisation (level 3) to dedicated hardware (level 4). Built-in agent sandboxes should be evaluated, not assumed sufficient (part d).</li>\n<li>Agents may find and exploit configuration issues in controls, so use several layers of isolation and re-validate configurations regularly, and include explicit prompts not to leave the allowlist or attempt escape (part d).</li>\n<li>Do not rely on prompting alone. Combine prompts with technical and operational controls, and repeat critical constraints in long-running tasks because context compression may drop them (section \"Prompt carefully\").</li>\n<li>Write down which activities are in scope and which are red lines, and carry out threat modelling before deployment (section \"Identify what could go wrong\").</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Name individuals or a group responsible for agentic AI activity where unintended activity would have significant consequences (section \"Set the right level of oversight\").</li>\n<li>Choose between human-in-the-loop, human-on-the-loop and human-out-of-the-loop according to risk tolerance. For higher risk, combine human oversight with technically enforced controls. Approvals should be \"guaranteed and gated\" (sections \"Prompt carefully\" and \"Set the right level of oversight\").</li>\n<li>Judge models may help supervise agents but must themselves be evaluated independently (section \"Set the right level of oversight\").</li>\n<li>Log agent transcripts, reasoning traces and sandbox events such as access logs, proxy logs and network traffic. Protect the logs from modification or deletion and make them immutable where possible (section \"Observability\").</li>\n<li>Treat agent activity as user activity within security operations and incident response. Start autonomous runs in office hours when more oversight is available, and extend to nights and weekends only once controls are shown to work (section \"Observability\").</li>\n<li>Keep the ability to \"pull the plug\" at once, which includes cutting network access and the link between agent and inference service, not only stopping the agent process (section \"Emergency shutdown\").</li>\n<li>Review controls and assumptions continually so that granted autonomy stays proportionate to risk appetite (section \"Further reading\").</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a short blog post, explicitly interim, with no empirical evaluation of the controls it recommends.</li>\n<li>It is aimed at designers and operators building highly autonomous agent environments. The higher maturity levels (dedicated hardware, locally hosted models) may be disproportionate for a small company.</li>\n<li>It refers to recent incidents involving unsanctioned agent activity through a linked NCSC statement, but this summary did not read that statement.</li>\n<li>It does not cover research integrity, data protection or EU legal obligations. The retrieval note (Retrieved 2026-11-05) in the file header is the compiler's, not the publisher's.</li>\n</ul>","rules":["r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-20","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-21","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-28","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-31","r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-32"]},{"id":"RIS-AT_2026-11-05_dsg-datenschutzbeauftragter","title":"Datenschutzgesetz (DSG), provisions on the data protection officer (excerpt)","publisher":"Bundeskanzleramt, RIS","date":"2026-11-05 (consolidated version of an Act from 1999, exception)","year":"2026","category":"Datenschutz","url":"https://www.ris.bka.gv.at/GeltendeFassung.wxe?Abfrage=Bundesnormen&Gesetzesnummer=10001597","binding":"Pflicht (law)","dateNote":"Fassung vom 05.11.2026 (last amendment listed: BGBl. I Nr. 50/2025)","note":"Included as an approved exception to the 2026 date window, for the question of designating a data protection officer.","abstract":"The DSG adds no designation duty for private bodies. A text search of the whole Act found only the provisions listed here, so Art. 37 GDPR is the operative rule (Artikel 2, 1.","summary":"<h4>Core statement</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The DSG adds no designation duty for private bodies. A text search of the whole Act found only the provisions listed here, so Art. 37 GDPR is the operative rule (Artikel 2, 1. Hauptstück, § 5).</li>\n<li>§ 5 Abs. 1 imposes secrecy on the data protection officer and persons working for the officer, including after their activity ends.</li>\n<li>§ 5 Abs. 2 extends a statutory right to refuse testimony to the officer, with a seizure ban for the related files.</li>\n<li>§ 5 Abs. 3 to 5 concern the data protection officer \"im öffentlichen Bereich\", in particular freedom from instructions and federal ministries.</li>\n<li>§ 57 Abs. 1 requires \"jeder Verantwortliche\" to designate an officer, but within the 3. Hauptstück, which under § 36 Abs. 1 covers processing by competent authorities for criminal law, public security and national security purposes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Relevance to the Leitfrage</h4>\n<h5>Client projects</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>A client authority that is a public body under Art. 37(1)(a) GDPR needs an officer by GDPR. The DSG only adds the position rules in § 5 Abs. 3 to 5 (§ 5).</li>\n<li>Processing that DHCraft carries out for a university or archive under the GDPR does not fall under § 57, which is limited to criminal-law and security authorities (§ 36 Abs. 1, § 57 Abs. 1).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Research</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Secrecy and the testimony privilege in § 5 Abs. 1 and 2 would apply to an officer DHCraft appoints voluntarily. This is an inference from the wording \"Der Datenschutzbeauftragte\", which names no limit to mandatory cases (§ 5 Abs. 1, 2).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Programming</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>No provision of the DSG on the data protection officer addresses software or AI tooling (whole excerpt).</li>\n</ul>\n<h5>Organisation</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>DHCraft's decision rests on Art. 37 GDPR, and the DSG adds secrecy duties for any officer it appoints (§ 5 Abs. 1).</li>\n<li>The Datengeheimnis in § 6 binds controllers, processors and their staff regardless of an officer. It lies outside the excerpt and was read only in part (§ 6 Abs. 1, 2).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The file is an excerpt. Other sections that mention the officer, such as the media privilege in § 9 and the Datenschutzrat composition, are not reproduced.</li>\n<li>RIS states the version date as the retrieval day, so earlier wording requires the amendment register.</li>\n<li>The classification of § 5 Abs. 1 and 2 as applying to voluntary officers in the private sector is an inference, not a statement of the text.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[]}],"rules":[{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-2","text":"Before an agent works with client data, document which service receives which data and in which role (controller, processor).","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Projekte","sources":[{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[33]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-3","text":"When agents replace earlier means of processing, update the record of processing activities with categories, recipients, transfers and retention.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Projekte","sources":[{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[36]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-4","text":"Check before each new agent use with personal data whether it needs its own legal basis and whether a data protection impact assessment is required.","status":"Pflicht","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Projekte","sources":[{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[35]},{"id":"DSB-AT_2026_newsletter-1-2026-ki-und-datenschutz","label":"DSB 1/2026","pages":[4]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-5","text":"Send personal data only to services bound as processors. For sensitive material use a local agent or a contractually secured service that excludes training use and logging by the provider.","status":"Ableitung","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Projekte","sources":[{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[31]},{"id":"DFG_2026-03_leitlinie-ki-in-der-begutachtung","label":"DFG 2026","pages":[2]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-6","text":"Give agents access only to the folders and accounts of the current task, never a whole mailbox, drive or customer database.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"sicher","field":"Projekte","sources":[{"id":"FiveEyes_2026-05-01_careful-adoption-agentic-ai","label":"Five Eyes 2026","pages":[7]},{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[19,55]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-7","text":"Tell clients which AI tools and agents handle their material and how it is protected.","status":"Ableitung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Projekte","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[10]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-8","text":"An agent that DHCraft builds and that talks to people discloses that it is an AI system and on whose behalf it acts. The duty lies with the provider, which is whoever puts the agent into service under their own name, DHCraft or the client who had it developed, so the contract settles this role.","status":"Pflicht","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Projekte","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission_2026-07-20_guidelines-article-50-transparency","label":"Art. 50 Guidelines 2026","pages":[6,13]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-9","text":"Internal texts and professional correspondence written with AI need no AI label. A text published to inform the public on matters of public interest needs either a human editorial review with a responsible person or a label.","status":"Pflicht","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Projekte","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission_2026-07-20_guidelines-article-50-transparency","label":"Art. 50 Guidelines 2026","pages":[43]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-10","text":"Disclose substantial AI use in FWF proposals. The FWF exempts literature reviews, language editing and translation, while the ERA guidelines count an AI literature review as substantial use, so disclosing it is the safer choice.","status":"Vorgabe","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Forschung","sources":[{"id":"FWF_2026-07-07_ki-in-foerderantraegen","label":"FWF 2026","pages":[]},{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[7]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-11","text":"Check every reference and factual statement an agent produced against the original source before it enters a proposal or publication. Hallucinated references in FWF proposals are sanctioned.","status":"Vorgabe","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Forschung","sources":[{"id":"FWF_2026-07-07_ki-in-foerderantraegen","label":"FWF 2026","pages":[]},{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[9]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-12","text":"Set check points in agent-driven research workflows at which a person validates methodological decisions. Responsibility stays with the researcher.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Forschung","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[7,8]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-13","text":"For research steps an agent performs, keep prompts, logs, model versions and parameters such as seeds and temperature, and decide whether to share them.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Forschung","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[7]},{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[66]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-14","text":"Pass third parties' personal data to external AI services only with consent or another legal basis and a clear purpose.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Forschung","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[8]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-15","text":"Do not rely on the AI Act research exemption for off-the-shelf agents, since it covers only systems developed for the sole purpose of scientific research.","status":"Ableitung","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Forschung","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission_2026-07-20_guidelines-article-50-transparency","label":"Art. 50 Guidelines 2026","pages":[9]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-16","text":"In peer review, keep the judgement human, disclose AI support with its purposes, and upload confidential material only to secured or local systems. For DFG reviews this is binding.","status":"Vorgabe","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Forschung","sources":[{"id":"DFG_2026-03_leitlinie-ki-in-der-begutachtung","label":"DFG 2026","pages":[2]},{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[9]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-17","text":"Publish code and describe methods precisely, because underspecified methods are the main obstacle when agents reproduce results.","status":"Ableitung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Forschung","sources":[{"id":"arXiv_2026-09-29_agentic-reproduction-social-science","label":"Agentic reproduction 2026","pages":[1]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-18","text":"No agent combines untrusted input, access to sensitive data and autonomous action. Where all three would meet, a human approves before the agent acts.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"sicher","field":"Programmierung","sources":[{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[39]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-19","text":"Treat web pages, mails and documents from third parties as possible carriers of hidden instructions for the agent.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"sicher","field":"Programmierung","sources":[{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[49]},{"id":"FiveEyes_2026-05-01_careful-adoption-agentic-ai","label":"Five Eyes 2026","pages":[6]},{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[13]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-20","text":"Run coding agents in a sandbox with network access restricted to an allowlist and with their own short-lived credentials.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"sicher","field":"Programmierung","sources":[{"id":"NCSC-UK_2026-08-20_managing-cyber-risk-agentic-ai","label":"NCSC-UK 2026","pages":[]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-21","text":"Deletions, deployments, pushes and outgoing messages require human approval that the tool enforces through its permission settings. An instruction in the prompt is insufficient.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"sicher","field":"Programmierung","sources":[{"id":"FiveEyes_2026-05-01_careful-adoption-agentic-ai","label":"Five Eyes 2026","pages":[22]},{"id":"NCSC-UK_2026-08-20_managing-cyber-risk-agentic-ai","label":"NCSC-UK 2026","pages":[]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-22","text":"Load only approved tools and extensions in fixed versions.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"sicher","field":"Programmierung","sources":[{"id":"FiveEyes_2026-05-01_careful-adoption-agentic-ai","label":"Five Eyes 2026","pages":[18]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-23","text":"Comments, issues and messages that an agent writes to people state that an AI agent wrote them and on whose behalf.","status":"Ableitung","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Programmierung","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission_2026-07-20_guidelines-article-50-transparency","label":"Art. 50 Guidelines 2026","pages":[13,15]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-24","text":"Keep agent changes small and agreed with the people who review them, because large and uncoordinated agent pull requests fail more often.","status":"Ableitung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Programmierung","sources":[{"id":"arXiv_2026-01-21_failed-agentic-pull-requests","label":"Failed agentic PRs 2026","pages":[1,2]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-25","text":"When learning a new library or method, use the agent for explanations and keep writing the code yourself, so that the competence needed to review agent code grows.","status":"Ableitung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Programmierung","sources":[{"id":"arXiv_2026-02-01_ai-impact-skill-formation","label":"Skill formation study 2026","pages":[1]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-26","text":"Take measures that support the AI literacy of everyone who works with agents for DHCraft, freelancers and contractors included. No certificate or guaranteed level is required.","status":"Pflicht","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Organisation","sources":[{"id":"EU-Parlament-Rat_2026-07-08_verordnung-2026-1744-digital-omnibus-ki","label":"Reg. 2026/1744","pages":[16]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-27","text":"Record in writing whether DHCraft must designate a data protection officer, and repeat the check when a new kind of project with personal data starts. The duty arises only where large-scale processing of special categories or large-scale monitoring is a core activity, for processors as well.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"regelkonform","field":"Organisation","sources":[{"id":"Art29WP_2017-04-05_wp243-guidelines-dpo","label":"WP 243","pages":[5,9]},{"id":"EU-Parlament-Rat_2016-04-27_dsgvo-art-37-39","label":"GDPR","pages":[]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-28","text":"Name a person responsible for DHCraft's agent use.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Organisation","sources":[{"id":"NCSC-UK_2026-08-20_managing-cyber-risk-agentic-ai","label":"NCSC-UK 2026","pages":[]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-29","text":"Keep a register of approved AI tools and agents with their uses, hosting location and terms on data reuse.","status":"Ableitung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Organisation","sources":[{"id":"EU-Kommission-GD-RTD_2026-10-15_era-living-guidelines-genai","label":"ERA Guidelines 2026","pages":[11]},{"id":"FiveEyes_2026-05-01_careful-adoption-agentic-ai","label":"Five Eyes 2026","pages":[18]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-30","text":"Introduce agents in stages, starting with low-risk tasks without sensitive data, and widen access only after experience.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"sicher","field":"Organisation","sources":[{"id":"FiveEyes_2026-05-01_careful-adoption-agentic-ai","label":"Five Eyes 2026","pages":[4,19]},{"id":"AEPD_2026-02_agentic-artificial-intelligence","label":"AEPD 2026","pages":[55]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-31","text":"Keep agent transcripts and protect them from modification, including by the agent.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"nachprüfbar","field":"Organisation","sources":[{"id":"NCSC-UK_2026-08-20_managing-cyber-risk-agentic-ai","label":"NCSC-UK 2026","pages":[]},{"id":"FiveEyes_2026-05-01_careful-adoption-agentic-ai","label":"Five Eyes 2026","pages":[20]}]},{"id":"r-leitlinien-dhcraft-c-32","text":"Keep the ability to stop all agent activity at once.","status":"Empfehlung","goal":"sicher","field":"Organisation","sources":[{"id":"NCSC-UK_2026-08-20_managing-cyber-risk-agentic-ai","label":"NCSC-UK 2026","pages":[]}]}]};
